Each variant removes a share of the rows with the highest scores (the step has only a `max_val=`, so
it's the highest that are removed; `remove="lowest"` would remove the lowest instead). The curve is
lowest at 2%, which is the share of flights with their air times in hours: removing fewer leaves
some of them in, and removing more starts to remove good flights. By 20%, the sweep hurts. A sweep
can also use the values of a column of the table as the scores (`sweep_score("column_name")`), or
cutoffs on the scale of the scores rather than shares of the rows (`values=`).

## Injecting Defects

The variants so far remove rows that a check flags, which asks whether the flagged rows hurt the
model. The opposite question is just as useful: if a defect got into the data, how much would it
hurt? The [`Ablation.inject()`](`Ablation.inject`) method answers it by adding the defect to
clean rows (those that fail no step) and evaluating the corrupted table. The defect can be one of
four kinds:

- `"null"`: the values of a column are made missing
- `"shuffle"`: the values of a column are moved between rows, so each is a valid value in the
  wrong row
- `"truncate"`: text values are cut to half their length
- `"duplicate"`: rows are copied

or a function, which is applied to the values of a column. Here, the defect is the problem that
step 1 catches, air times in hours, made by a function that divides the air time by 60:

::: {#c9f24624 .cell execution_count=13}
``` {.python .cell-code}
injected = (
    pb.Ablation(
        validation,
        evaluate=evaluate,
        metric="mae",
        higher_is_better=False,
        label="Air time model",
    )
    .inject(lambda minutes: minutes / 60, step=1, name="hours", rates=[0.01, 0.02, 0.05, 0.1])
    .run()
)

injected
```

