:::


The scorers of scikit-learn are such that higher is better (which is why the mean absolute error
is negated), so the default `higher_is_better=True` is the right one here. With this model,
removing the flights with a zero distance helps too. Whether a problem in the data matters depends
on the model, which is why an ablation is worth running with the model that will be trained on the
data. Pass `estimator=` to use another model or pipeline: it's cloned for each evaluation, with its
`random_state` set to the seed.

Every value in this report has a standard deviation of `± 0.000`, since the gradient boosting model
gives the same result whatever its seed. Its repeats differ only in the controls, which remove a
new set of random rows each time. The repeats matter most with a model that's random itself, like
the random forest, whose results vary from seed to seed. Either way, the
[`Ablation.check_determinism()`](`Ablation.check_determinism`) method (see below) checks that the
same seed gives the same result.

## Checking the Held-Out Data

The held-out data is the yardstick of every comparison, so a problem in it is a problem in all of
them: a model that's scored on bad data can be rewarded for learning the same mistakes. With
`holdout=`, the `Ablation` checks the held-out data with the same steps as the training table (it's
never ablated, only checked), and the plan and the report give the health score of each:

::: {#9885ba80 .cell execution_count=21}
``` {.python .cell-code}
checked = pb.Ablation(
    validation,
    evaluate=evaluate,
    metric="mae",
    higher_is_better=False,
    holdout=holdout,
).drop_each()

checked.plan()
```

